{"parentUuid":"02e42daf-4e61-4e94-8803-36815297f1ce","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdNxnynmcaKQa3RsZaSUd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is questioning whether SQLite for the execution register is overkill compared to the existing append-only JSONL pattern with state folding. The execution register is really just a read-optimized projection for comparing task performance across different models and machines — so I need to think through whether that justifies the added complexity.\n\nSQLite does have genuine advantages here: R16 queries need cross-machine, cross-time analytical comparisons with GROUP BY, JOINs, and range scans, which SQL handles well. And at LONGVIEW scale with potentially hundreds of thousands of execution records, folding a massive JSONL into memory for each query becomes inefficient — indexes make those comparisons cheap. But the user's instinct is valid too: SQLite breaks the architectural elegance of \"one doctrine, N logs\" where everything else is JSONL plus chain-hash plus fold, and introducing a separate database feels like a departure from that principle.\n\nThe real tension is that the register is a projection — it's rebuildable from the four source logs, so it's a cache, not truth. The question becomes whether you actually need a SQL cache or if in-memory folding is sufficient. Adding SQLite also means maintaining two schema-evolution stories: the converter registry for JSONL and ALTER TABLE migrations for the database. The existing dashboard is deliberately file-based and deterministic, sourced from JSONL, so introducing SQL adds a second query paradigm to a codebase that's avoided databases entirely. JSON on disk is also more inspectable and recoverable than a binary SQLite file if corruption happens.\n\nLooking at what the requirements actually demand — comparison queries across machines and time, per-execution metrics — these are analytical