{"parentUuid":"bb8ed4ee-123f-47a1-a292-dcd3bf741b76","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdNyeiqzMBviSU9CDLrLJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explaining the four open decisions I've asked you to rule on — what each one actually is, why it's a genuine choice, and where I land.\n\n**1. KEL (and staging) live on D:, not C:/the home clone**\n\nThe knowledge event log is *truth* — the thing every store rebuilds from. Two places it could live:\n- **On D:** (the portable external drive, `D:\\flywheel-staging`). Truth travels with the corpus, is the same physical thing across machines, and is what R41 backup/restore targets.\n- **On C:/inside the benny home** and merely *synced* to D:.\n\nThe tension: D: is an external drive — it's not always attached, and it's a single point of failure until L3 (durability/replication) lands. Putting truth on it is exactly why R41 (backup + checksum + restore drill) is a wave-1 task and not deferred. The alternative (truth on C:, D: as transport) keeps truth on a more-always-present disk but breaks the \"one physical truth across machines\" property and reintroduces a machine dependency (R20 says no machine need be online). I chose D: because the requirements are explicit that the portable drive is *the single point of truth for raw* (R17) and local-first/portable is a hard constraint (R21/R34). **My lean: keep it on D:, with L3 as the mandatory guard.** Worth your confirmation because it makes \"is D: attached?\" a hard precondition for the loop.\n\n**2. Epic letter `EP-L` and milestone `M4`**\n\nTwo naming choices with a mechanical consequence:\n- **`EP-L`** — epics A/B/C/D/E/F/G/Q/R/T/W are taken; L (for LONGVIEW/flywheel) is free and mnemonic. Low stakes; rename if you have a convention I'm missing.\n- **`M4`** — this is the real one. The `SPEC-work-contracts` validator hard-codes the milestone field as `M1` or `M2`. But EP-T already uses M3 (so the spec text is already behind reality), and this is net-new large work that shouldn't be crammed into an existing milestone. Introducing M4 means the `w0` gate's milestone enum needs an **additive bump** (`M1|M2` → `M1|M2|M3|M4`). I folded that one-line change into L0's allowlist. It's itself a steer-10 additive schema change — pleasingly self-consistent, but you should know the flywheel's first task edits a shared validator. **My lean: `EP-L` + `M4`, with the enum bump in L0.**\n\n**3. The `human-signed` vs `agent-ok` authority split across L0–L14**\n\nEvery W0 contract declares `authority`: `agent-ok` (any agent can claim and run it autonomously) or `human-signed` (needs your signature to proceed). This encodes your human-signed-stops doctrine into the board. I marked as **`human-signed`**: L0 (the KEL schema — the substrate's constitution), L1 (staging layout on the truth drive), L3 (durability — data-loss surface), L9 (privacy-honoring history), and all of L10–L13 (the loop daemon, collapse guard, model promotion, decision function — anything that trains or swaps the served model). I left as **`agent-ok`**: L2 (poison gate), L4 (delta engine), L5 (register), L6 (tagging), L7 (claim/compaction), L14 (dashboard) — mechanical-but-bounded substrate work. The judgment call is *where the line falls*: too much `human-signed` and you're a bottleneck on your own board; too little and an agent autonomously reshapes truth or promotes a model. **These are my read of your doctrine, not a steer — adjust any you'd draw differently.**\n\n**4. The sharp one: should L0 *itself* be `human-signed`?**\n\nL0 defines the knowledge event log's schema — the format all truth is written in. I set it `human-signed` because getting the envelope wrong is the most expensive mistake in the whole design (every later record inherits it). But there's a real cost: §8's delivery doctrine says *dogfood the flywheel — let the house model carry high-volume work and stage those sessions as training material*. A `human-signed` L0 means the substrate's foundational contract is authored by a frontier session under your signature, **not** by the house model. That's almost certainly correct (you don't want the house model defining the truth format on turn one, and R38's collapse guard exists precisely to keep raw self-output out of the constitution) — but it does mean the highest-value \"method-in-weights\" signal (frontier *correcting* house work, R25) doesn't get generated on L0. **My lean: keep L0 human-signed;** the schema is exactly the thing that shouldn't be dogfooded early. Flagging it only because it's the one place the two doctrines (human-signed-stops vs. dogfood-the-house-model) actively pull against each other.\n\nWant me to walk through anything deeper — or is one of these where you'd steer differently?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4,"cache_read_input_tokens":128163,"output_tokens":2160,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2160,"cache_read_input_tokens":128163,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdNyeddTuJuqbg4xgsC1p","type":"assistant","uuid":"1a833d01-1d76-4c41-826f-6b5ea52e4b02","timestamp":"2026-07-25T15:02:29.318Z","effort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16","sessionId":"468ee845-7d82-4724-a78a-d2a9fbbb431e","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
