essions and drives from a source... The function should return a summary object with counts of what was stored, deduplicated, processed, and skipped. For true delta-only behavior on re-sync, I need to gate the estate_drive emission itself — if nothing changed (no new sessions processed and the drive fingerprint stayed the same), skip emitting that event too, rather than always emitting it. This means tracking the fingerprint across syncs to detect actual changes.\n\nThe cleaner approach is to treat the drive manifest like session inputs: feed its fingerprint through processDelta with a dedicated stage, so it only emits an event when the fingerprint is new. This way, an unchanged re-sync produces zero events across the board.\n\nDefining syncSource to handle this...\n\nHandling rebuild identity...\n\nFor machines, I'll emit an estate_machine event during the initial sync of each machine, keyed by the machine and role fingerprint. I'm setting up the core estate module with event type constants, builder functions for session/drive/machine events that return envelopes, a fold function that constructs the estate object from events, a deterministic serializer for stable JSON output, and a utility to rebuild the estate file from the event log.\n\nNow I'm building the sync layer that orchestrates the casStore, processDelta, and event builders together. The test suite will cover four BDD scenarios plus a mutation test where breaking the dedup key—by keying sessions on sid instead of content hash—causes two drives with identical content but different sids to create duplicate session entries, which the overlap dedup test should catch.\n\nFor determinism, buildEstate must avoid timestamps and derive state purely from event payloads, with serializeEstate sorting keys to ensure rebuilding from the same events produces byte-identical output. The events themselves get txn_time at sync creation, but since rebuild reads the same log, that's consistent.\n\nWriting estate.mjs... Defini